1295		The line-point distribution places weight $\tfrac{1}{2}$ on each of the
1296		axis-parallel and diagonal components and, within each component, uniform
1297		weight on the index $i$, so each restricted distribution carries mixture
1298		weight $\tfrac{1}{2m}$ in $D_{\mathrm{Line}}$. A nonnegative average that
1299		is at most $\delta$ therefore bounds each mixture component by
1300		$2m\delta$, and products of two such mixtures give the factor
1301		$(2m)^2 = 4m^2$. The instance \eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} is item
1302		2 applied to the conclusion of Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines}, whose
1303		$\simeq_{\delta_P}$ relation over two independent copies of the
1304		line-point distribution is precisely the statement that the left-hand
1305		side of \eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}, averaged over
1306		$D_{\mathrm{Line}} \times D_{\mathrm{Line}}$, is at most $\delta_P$.
1307	\end{proof}
